\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{Odd vectors carry twice the dot form}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{On the periodic cubical torus, take both directions of every
axis and give every weight the value #{1}. At each vertex the
derived form of \ref{fdip-00PB} is the dot form on a free fibre
of rank #{2d}, by \ref{fdip-00PF}. Write #{e_{i+}} for the
forward edge of axis #{i} and #{e_{i-}} for the backward edge.
The odd vector #{v_i=e_{i+}-e_{i-}} and the even vector
#{w_i=e_{i+}+e_{i-}} satisfy
##{q(v_i)=q(w_i)=2,\qquad B(v_i,w_i)=0,}
and vectors of distinct axes are orthogonal. For an affine
function in the sense of \ref{fdip-00PK},
##{e_x\,df=\sum_i a_i v_i.}
On the odd vectors the derived form equals #{2\bar q}, where
#{\bar q\bigl(\sum_i c_i\varepsilon_i\bigr)=\sum_i c_i^2} is the
dot form defined by the weights. No step divides by #{2}. The
odd and even vectors span a submodule whose quotient is
isomorphic to #{(k/(2))^d}. Over #{\mathbb{Z}} the index is
#{2^d}. The identification of #{v_i} with #{\varepsilon_i} is
stated on that submodule. Passing from the odd and even vectors back to the
original edges uses the inverse of #{2}. The operator #{D} of
\ref{fdip-00OL} pairs the two directions: its coboundary reads
the forward edges and its adjoint reads the backward edges.}

\proof{
\p{The form is diagonal on the edges, with every entry #{1}, so
#{q(e_{i+}-e_{i-})=1+1} and the cross term of distinct edges is
#{0}. The same holds for the even vector, and
#{B(v_i,w_i)=1-1=0}. An affine function has forward difference
#{a_i} and backward difference #{-a_i}, which is the stated
odd combination. Therefore
#{q\bigl(\sum_i c_i v_i\bigr)=\sum_i c_i^2\cdot 2=2\bar q}.}

\p{In one axis, the coordinates of #{v_i} and #{w_i} in the
edge basis are the columns of
##{\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
=\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\-1&1\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}1&0\\0&2\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\0&1\end{pmatrix}.}
The outer factors have determinant #{1}. The middle factor
has quotient isomorphic to #{k/(2)}. The axes are independent,
so the quotient is isomorphic to #{(k/(2))^d}. Over
#{\mathbb{Z}} the index is #{2^d}. Solving for the original edges gives
#{e_{i+}=(w_i+v_i)/2} and #{e_{i-}=(w_i-v_i)/2}, which are
the steps that need #{2} to be a unit. \ref{fdip-00OT} writes
the coboundary as the sum of the forward differences and the
adjoint as the sum of the backward differences, so #{D}
pairs each axis.}
}
